% ============================================================================
% em-spectrum — the seven bands of the electromagnetic spectrum and a
% representative everyday use / thing NASA associates with each one
% (adj-facts-stdlib, PHYSICS).
% ============================================================================
% The electromagnetic spectrum is the same physical thing — light — split by
% wavelength into named bands, from the longest-wavelength radio waves to the
% shortest-wavelength, most energetic gamma rays. Between them lie microwaves,
% infrared, visible light, ultraviolet, and X-rays. This is a rung a PHYSICS (or
% ASTRONOMY, or MEDICINE) agent reasons UP from: before you can talk about a
% telescope's detector or an X-ray machine you must first know WHICH band you are
% in. A famous way to make the seven bands memorable is to attach to each one a
% familiar, everyday thing that uses or is associated with it: "radio waves come
% from radio stations, microwaves cook, infrared is night vision, our eyes see
% visible light, ultraviolet tans skin, X-rays image teeth, and gamma rays are
% used for medical imaging inside the body." Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? band_use(microwave, $Application)      % cooking
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `band_use(band, application)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the use
% WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on anything
% that is not one of the seven EM bands (it never invents a use).
%
% Each of the seven bands and the representative everyday use / association NASA
% states for it, as a little truth table:
%
%     band          everyday use / association         a beginner's cue
%     ----------    -------------------------------    -------------------------
%     radio         radio_stations                     your radio catches radio waves
%     microwave     cooking                            a microwave cooks popcorn
%     infrared      night_vision                       goggles pick up body heat
%     visible       eyes                               the only band our eyes detect
%     ultraviolet   tanning                            the Sun's UV tans and burns skin
%     x_ray         teeth                              a dentist images your teeth
%     gamma_ray     medical_imaging                    doctors see inside your body
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a use and recall its band:
%
%     ? band_use($Band, night_vision)   % infrared
%
% A word that is NOT one of the seven EM bands — `sound` (a mechanical wave, not
% light at all), `seismic`, `rock` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a band recall
% ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a use. That honest-abstention property is
% what makes the table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the
% band->use pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `application` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be a
% representative everyday use / association the source EXPLICITLY names for that
% band — never one the source does not support. (Where the everyday word differs
% from the source's exact word — the source says "cook your popcorn", not
% "cooking"; "gamma-ray imaging ... inside your body", not "medical imaging" —
% the atom is a light normalization of the source's own noun, so every value
% stays checkable against the quoted span below.)
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every band->use pair below
% was read out of a fetched NASA "Introduction to the Electromagnetic Spectrum"
% page, and any pair whose use could not be verified there would have been
% dropped). Each pair, with the primary source it was copied from and the
% verbatim span that states it:
%
%   radio -> radio_stations
%     https://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/science/toolbox/emspectrum1.html
%     (NASA Goddard Space Flight Center — Imagine the Universe!)
%     "Your radio captures radio waves emitted by radio stations, bringing your
%      favorite tunes."
%
%   microwave -> cooking
%     https://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/science/toolbox/emspectrum1.html
%     (NASA Goddard Space Flight Center — Imagine the Universe!)
%     "Microwave radiation will cook your popcorn in just a few minutes, but is
%      also used by astronomers to learn about the structure of nearby galaxies."
%
%   infrared -> night_vision
%     https://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/science/toolbox/emspectrum1.html
%     (NASA Goddard Space Flight Center — Imagine the Universe!)
%     "Night vision goggles pick up the infrared light emitted by our skin and
%      objects with heat."
%
%   visible -> eyes
%     https://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/science/toolbox/emspectrum1.html
%     (NASA Goddard Space Flight Center — Imagine the Universe!)
%     "Our eyes detect visible light."
%     (The page continues "Fireflies, light bulbs, and stars all emit visible
%      light."; the ROW ships only the first sentence, which is the one that
%      states the use. An earlier version of this listing quoted both, so the
%      header and the shipped span disagreed about which span warrants the row.)
%
%   ultraviolet -> tanning
%     https://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/science/toolbox/emspectrum1.html
%     (NASA Goddard Space Flight Center — Imagine the Universe!)
%     "Ultraviolet radiation is emitted by the Sun and are the reason skin tans
%      and burns."
%
%   x_ray -> teeth
%     https://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/science/toolbox/emspectrum1.html
%     (NASA Goddard Space Flight Center — Imagine the Universe!)
%     "A dentist uses X-rays to image your teeth, and airport security uses them
%      to see through your bag."
%
%   gamma_ray -> medical_imaging
%     https://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/science/toolbox/emspectrum1.html
%     (NASA Goddard Space Flight Center — Imagine the Universe!)
%     "Doctors use gamma-ray imaging to see inside your body."
%
% EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SPAN as its primary warrant (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e), and
% the `source`/`locator`/`trust` envelope below holds the page's FRAMING
% sentence, which names no band. THE SPANS IN THE TABLE ARE THE AUTHORITY; the
% listing above is commentary, and where the two differ the table wins.
%
% Until issue #14986 was applied here, this paragraph read: "An ADJ `table`
% carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/`trust` below hold
% the single cleanest span: the NASA statement that fixes the first row (radio
% -> radio_stations). Every OTHER row's per-fact source and verbatim span is
% listed above." That was true of the old shape and is false of this one, and
% an auditor reading top-down met it before reaching the table. A provenance
% artifact whose prose contradicts its data is the exact failure mode RS-5e
% exists to remove, so it is recorded here rather than quietly overwritten.
%
% All seven rows come from the SAME primary source, NASA Goddard Space Flight
% Center's "Imagine the Universe!" education page (imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov), a
% primary U.S. government teaching resource, so `trust authoritative`. No row
% restates `locator`: every span is on that one page, which is the rule
% ADJ-TABLES.md §4 states.
%
% "VERBATIM" HERE IS CONDITIONAL ON ONE STRIPPING RULE, and it should be stated
% rather than assumed: three of the eight spans straddle an inline
% `<a class="glossaryDef">` anchor in the page source — the ENVELOPE ("EM
% spectrum"), the MICROWAVE span ("astronomers", "galaxies") and the VISIBLE
% span ("light"). The anchors wrap plain text, so stripping tags with NO
% separator reproduces the page's own characters exactly; the other five spans
% are contiguous in the raw bytes. Checked 2026-09-14 (HTTP 200): all eight
% occur exactly once each, wholly inside one `<p>`, and a fabricated control
% sentence is reported absent by the same comparison.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table band_use {
    columns band, application

    % Each row carries the sentence that names ITS OWN BAND AND ITS USE.
    % Until issue #14986 was applied here, the RADIO
    % sentence was the envelope `source` and therefore the warrant for
    % all seven rows -- so `? band_use(x_ray, $A)` came back proved by a
    % sentence about radio stations. Six of the seven were in that
    % position.
    %
    % NO CROSS-ROW OVERLAP: measured against the page's own wording for
    % each key (`x_ray` is "X-rays", `gamma_ray` is "gamma-ray"), each of
    % the seven spans names its own band and NO other. There is therefore
    % no pairing trap here of the kind `anatomy/eye-parts.adj` carries,
    % where the LENS sentence names the cornea and the retina, the
    % OPTIC-NERVE sentence names the retina, and the IRIS sentence names
    % the pupil.
    %
    % (An earlier wording called this "unusual in this cascade". IT IS
    % NOT, and the claim was made without looking. Surveying the
    % converted files: eleven others already have all-distinct spans, and
    % `biology/plant-tropisms.adj`, `physics/energy-forms.adj` and
    % `science/scientific-method-step.adj` also have zero cross-naming.
    % The contrast with `eye-parts` is real; the rarity was not. Note too
    % that `earth-science/water-cycle.adj` is NOT a
    % converted-but-overlapping table — it has no row-level blocks at
    % all, and is blocked for a different reason: its value is an integer
    % that no span on its page states.)
    %
    % NO ROW RESTATES `locator`: all seven sentences are on the one page
    % the envelope names, which is the rule `geography/reference-lines.adj`
    % states.
    row (radio, radio_stations) {
        source "Your radio captures radio waves emitted by radio stations, bringing your favorite tunes."
    }
    row (microwave, cooking) {
        source "Microwave radiation will cook your popcorn in just a few minutes, but is also used by astronomers to learn about the structure of nearby galaxies."
    }
    row (infrared, night_vision) {
        source "Night vision goggles pick up the infrared light emitted by our skin and objects with heat."
    }
    row (visible, eyes) {
        source "Our eyes detect visible light."
    }
    row (ultraviolet, tanning) {
        source "Ultraviolet radiation is emitted by the Sun and are the reason skin tans and burns."
    }
    row (x_ray, teeth) {
        source "A dentist uses X-rays to image your teeth, and airport security uses them to see through your bag."
    }
    row (gamma_ray, medical_imaging) {
        source "Doctors use gamma-ray imaging to see inside your body."
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE IS THE PAGE'S OWN INTRODUCTION TO PRECISELY THIS LIST,
    % and it names no band, so it cannot mis-warrant a row. Checked
    % against all seven row keys in the page's own wording.
    source "The image below shows where you might encounter each portion of the EM spectrum in your day-to-day life."
    locator "https://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/science/toolbox/emspectrum1.html"
    trust authoritative
}
